> Choosing a Character  

This section should be before What You Should Do [the short version]

Also it should contain useful advice for choosing a character.

The easiest two classes are V and B, for the (typical) 18/18 starting 
stats, speed, good weapon skills, good first artifact, etc.  Valks are 
slightly better, because they get stealth, the +3 shield, Mojo/Excalibur, 
and a great quest.  Barbs have poison res and a decent chance at starting 
with 18/01 STR.

The next two easiest are Samurai and Cavemen, but they are just weaker 
versions of the above two classes.  For fun, variety, and learning 
certain aspects of the game, Wizards and Priests are good choices.

Alignments:
Lawful: Can turn a long sword into Excalibur.  Otherwise awful.
Neutral: Several good choices for Quest Artifact wishes.
Chaotic: No penalty for murder.

> Avoiding Fights

A third reason to avoid fights:  The goal of the game is to obtain the 
amulet for your god.  Hunting down monsters usually does not advance that 
goal, yet exposes you to risk.  Most monsters do not have worthwhile 
items and do not give intrinsics.  You'll gain exp soon enough from the 
monsters who hunt _you_ down.  

> One of my favorite early wands is the wand of speed. I speed up
> both the pet and myself to make us both more effective.

Of course, all the easiest classes get speed for free at XL:1 or XL:7...

There is almost no mention of jumping boots, although they are as common 
as speed boots and nearly as good.  They're great for retreating from a 
tough monster while pelting it with missiles, or for melee-ing a large 
group of monsters in an open area, or just for running away at top speed.  
They let you escape pits instantly and "cheat" in doorways, even shop 
doorways.  They identify for free, of course.

> Stores

> both levels two and three are guaranteed to have a store if
> there is a room with only one door (and no stairs

room-343.txt: "in practice [this] turns out to only be the case about 45% 
of the time"  Some guarantee.

Pet-theft is overrated IMO, unless you're going to credit-clone.  I've 
seen some people have great success with credit-cloning.  I prefer to pay 
for the items I want right away, then come back later and steal 
everything with the book trick.

It is always better to drop gold on the ground rather than paying 
directly, because when you want the gold back it is a lot easier to get 
back it from the ground than from the shopkeep.

My views of the shops:

used armor dealership: Mostly crap.  Can raise some money here with junk 
from the mines.  Buy the good armors right away, come back later and 
steal the magical stuff for polypiling.

scroll and book stores: Most classes won't be able to make use of spells 
early.  Sell any known spellbooks here; you won't need them again until 
turn 20k.  Sell your unided or non-emergency scrolls.  When you are ready 
to use scrolls, come back and take everything.     

liquor emporium: A great place, especially the enormous ones.  Buy the 
expensive waters if you can afford them.  Buy the cheap ones too.  Buy 
the $50 potions if you need something to dilute into water.  Watch out 
for monsters who will come in and drink all the good potions.  Steal 
everything as soon as is feasible.  

delicatessen: Buy anything that gives intrinsics.  Buy and dilute any 
potions.  Otherwise not even worth robbing.

jewelers:  Another fun place.  Price-ID is especially important here.  
Steal everything (not the gems), then head to an altar for b/u/c.  The 
rings you really want are:
regeneration
invisibility
conflict
TC
poly+control is probably excellent, but I've never tried it.

Other, more situational, good ones:
poison resistance
gain constitution  
teleportation
stealth

levitation and free action will be useful eventually. The rest are 
polyfodder.

wand store: Easiest to just steal them all.

hardware store: Great place, often has excellent items that you can use 
right away like magic whistle, stethoscope, magic lamp...  Usually found 
in Minetown, where is is small enough that the simplest way is to just 
buy what you need.

  
